Deep learning techniques continuously evolve and gain widespread acceptance across
various scientific and industrial applications. Despite their popularity, the
fundamental operating principles underlying these techniques remain unclear, mainly
due to a lack of suitable mathematical frameworks that can effectively model and
interpret deep learning algorithms. This work establishes a rigorous mathematical
structure by adapting concepts from geometric topology, specifically focusing on
the topology of deep learning networks. By examining the topology of these
networks, we identify key features and develop metrics that enable the comparison
of different deep learning architectures. Furthermore, our approach facilitates the
application of established mathematical theorems to demonstrate the convergence
of deep learning networks. By incorporating topology from computer science and
establishing a formal mathematical structure, we bridge the gap between theoretical
mathematics and the practice of deep learning. This new mathematical framework
allows us to explore novel research avenues at the intersection of machine
learning, topology, and geometry. Ultimately, our work lays the groundwork for
fusing deep learning and topology to address various complex real-world challenges. 

\textit{Note: This research was conducted at the University of Texas.} 

