Beyond the basic principle of minimizing misclassification, the choice of loss function plays a significant role in deep learning training. This research explores how modifying loss functions impacts the performance of well-established classification models. Our investigation encompasses three primary areas: 
  (1) how adjusting loss functions affects model optimization;
  (2) how different loss functions are influenced by data imbalance; 
  (3) what are the most suitable and least suitable loss functions for integration with deep learning models for classification tasks.

Our analysis reveals that altering the loss function leads to varying degrees of impact on model performance, with some changes having a substantial drop-off and others remaining relatively stable. With regard to imbalanced data, our findings indicate that certain loss functions tend to favor the dominant class, whereas others have a greater tendency to predict the minority class.

