A geometric interpretation of geometric T-duality on a circle of radius $R$ with
flat connection $A$ and curvature $F$ involves translating the patch $\mathcal{M}$
defined by the equation $x \wedge F = 0$ and the set of points with radius $R$, into a
differently weighted space, $\mathcal{M}'$, using the map $\Phi$. This translation
provides a bundle isomorphism between the original space and a space with radius $1/R$.
This geometric interpretation applies to a number of situations, including: a circle
with no flux; a circle with magnetic monopole flux; a circle with differential form
flux. In all these scenarios, the T-dual of the space can be explicitly described. This
geometric interpretation cannot be extended to a holomorphic map, however, if the flux
is a complex differential form. A related result involves showing that the map $\Phi$
is not able to be extended into a holomorphic map when the flux is a complex
differential form. Furthermore, this record discusses the related general theory. 
